</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLMs</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate our proposed Bayesian model on two distinct architectures: a pre-trained 2.8b parameter Transformer model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and a pre-trained 2.8b parameter Mamba model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. Both LLMs use a sampling temperature of 0.75 to generate outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The prompts for each attack are each parsed to the LLMs 25 times each. Once attack successes have been counted, we then collect <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10000</annotation></semantics></math> samples from the posterior distributions using the importance sampling procedure described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Implementation ‣ IV Analysis: quantifying uncertainty ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To first investigate the statistical validity of the proposed Bayesian model, that is, the model’s ability to identify independent prompts and improve the quantification of uncertainty, we explore the model’s posterior cluster allocations in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-D Results ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Since the cluster assignments are random variables with posterior distributions, the plot shows the co-clustering probability via a posterior similarity matrix (PSM; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>); the proportion of times that prompts pairs belong to the same cluster according to the importance samples and weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-D Results ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the supplementary full text versions for the labels in Appendix <a href="#A0.SS3" title="-C Supplementary results for the model validation in Section ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-C</span></a>, the proposed model appears to group prompts by the target for the coding task. Thus, the Bayesian model appears to have identified prompts that arguably test similar areas of LLM robustness. Importantly, Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-D Results ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows clear between-cluster independence. The identification and independence of clusters validates the need to cluster prompt embeddings to trust quantification of uncertainty in these attacks. The PSM plots for the other attacks for both LLMs are presented in Appendix <a href="#A0.SS3" title="-C Supplementary results for the model validation in Section ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-C</span></a> (along with the full text versions of the axis labels) and show similar identification of independent clusters.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05709v1/figures/prompt_heatmap_packagehallucination_JavaScript.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/224;" width="333" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Cluster label PSM for the <em id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Package hallucination (JavaScript)</em> attack and 2.8b parameter Mamba model. The labels on the x axis are in the same order as those on the y axis. For presentation, the presented label orders are found via hierarchical clustering with average linkage, but this does not change the data presented.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the conceptual grounding of the reliability from embedding-space clustering, we now explore how the identification of independent prompts improves the accuracy of inference. In the absence of ground-truth attack success rates, we cannot directly validate conclusions against observed values. Instead, we can test if the proposed Bayesian model’s conclusions extend to unseen data via predictive accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, indicating the reliability of the underlying relationships found by the Bayesian model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
