<td id="S5.T2.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">clusters</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">clusters</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ansi escape (AnsiRaw)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-96.8</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">-567.3</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-169.4</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">-431.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Divergence (Repeat)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-100.1</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">-145.7</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-88.2</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">-126.7</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent injection (EnFr)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-220.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">-247.6</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.T2.2.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-183.6</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">-234.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S5.T2.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Package hallucination (JavaScript)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S5.T2.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-56.9</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">-87.6</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S5.T2.2.7.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D1F0B9;">-48.1</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">-74.9</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Approximate expected log predictive densities (ELPDs) for the attacks and LLMs considered. The best ELPDs are highlighted in green.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use 5-fold cross-validation with approximate stratification to demonstrate out-of-sample performance while avoiding biasing the results by introducing new classes at prediction time. We first coarsely label the prompts with approximate classes and attempt to ensure that the training set contains exactly one example of each class guess. The rest of the data are then partitioned into the folds. We note that coarse labelling would not be required in a real-world example and does not inform or bias the proposed model directly here. For each fold, we then find the joint posterior distribution of the unknown parameters given the training set, approximated by the importance samples from Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In V-A Attacks ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. These weighted Monte Carlo samples are then applied to approximate the expected log predictive density (ELPD). As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, the ELPD can be interpreted as a predictive likelihood of the observed value under each Bayesian model, capturing posterior uncertainty, making it an appropriate criterion for evaluating the fit of the posterior distributions. The approximate ELPDs from each LLM, attack and Bayesian model under test are presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-D Results ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>. Higher ELPD indicates that the posterior generalises better, thus supporting more reliable explanatory conclusions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-D Results ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> shows the favourable performance of the proposed model across the range of attacks and both LLMs considered. The difference in the ELPDs appears greatest for the <em id="S5.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ansi escape</em> attack. This correlates with the extent of inter-dependence between prompts and the size of the clusters found as per Appendix <a href="#A0.SS3" title="-C Supplementary results for the model validation in Section ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-C</span></a>, indicating that the proposed Bayesian model is particularly effective at improving the reliability of inference when there is strong inter-dependence between prompts. Conversely, smaller differences are observed for the attacks with more independent prompts, but even accounting for this lower level of inter-dependence has improved out-of-sample performance. We also note that predictive performance disadvantages the proposed model compared to the standard analysis scenario. We attempted to mitigate against some of this bias in our partitioning of training and test sets. However, for prediction, prompts that form new, unanticipated clusters must be allocated to an already-existing cluster, which we do via the closest average linkage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Case study: Mamba versus Transformers</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now demonstrate the proposed model and evaluation pipeline in practice, focusing on
assessing the vulnerabilities in Mamba models compared to Transformers. For brevity, we employ each of the attacks in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A Attacks ‣ V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> but note again that the extension to further attacks is immediate. Considering the two practical perspectives on LLM comparisons motivated by the rationale in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Experimental design: controlling confounding variables ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, we compare LLMs by training data and performance in turn.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparing LLMs by training</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As part of our effort to control for training data and thus provide practically-feasible insights on adversarially robust choices when training an LLM, we searched <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hugging Face</em> for base LLMs that are trained and specified as similarly as possible. We selected 9 LLMs in three groups, which include Mamba and Transformer models, but also hybrid models, reportedly combining the benefits of both architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. The inclusion of hybrid models allows us to explore the effect of mixing characteristics from different architecture types. The LLMs considered are presented in Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-A Comparing LLMs by training ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, with further details on the LLMs, including their full Hugging Face repository names, provided in Appendix <a href="#A0.SS4" title="-D Supplementary material for the case study in Section ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">-D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S6.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t"></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S6.T3.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_text">
