</span></span><span id="S6.T3.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group 1:</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Mamba</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">mamba-2.8b-hf</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S6.T3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the Pile</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Mamba-2</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">mamba2-2.7b</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S6.T3.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.7-2.8b</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Transformer</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">pythia-2.8b</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Transformer<math id="S6.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="++" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">++</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">transformerpp-2.7b</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.6.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Mamba-2-Transformer</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">mamba2attn-2.7b</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group 2:</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Mamba</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">falcon-mamba-7b</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S6.T3.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RefinedWeb</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Transformer</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">falcon-7b</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S6.T3.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7b</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.9.2" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.9.3" class="ltx_td"></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T3.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group 3:</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Transformer</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Llama-3.2-3B</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S6.T3.2.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Undisclosed</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Transformer-</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Llamba-3B</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b"><span id="S6.T3.2.12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Llama) 3b</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">Mamba-2 distilled</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_b"></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>LLM architectures for each set of training data, grouped by training data provided. Figures quoted alongside the training data are the number of parameters.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The implementation parameters are largely the same as those in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Bayesian model validation ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>. Again, we use a standard sampling temperature of 0.75 and parse the prompts to each LLM 25 times. Once attack successes have been counted, we then collect <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10000</annotation></semantics></math> samples from the posterior distributions using the importance sampling procedure described in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Implementation ‣ IV Analysis: quantifying uncertainty ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a>. We present the results in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ VI-A Comparing LLMs by training ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which shows the posterior means and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> credible intervals for each entire attack posterior, <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(p_{a}|x_{1:n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(p_{a}|x_{1:n})</annotation></semantics></math>, as described in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Analysis: quantifying uncertainty ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05709v1/figures/case_study_by_training.png" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/208;" width="476" height="208" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Results for each attack, showing average posterior means and <math id="S6.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> credible intervals. The x-axis labels can be cross-referenced with Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-A Comparing LLMs by training ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>. The run time for each LLM and attack combination, and 10000 importance samples is approximately 5 minutes on a single CPU.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One may be tempted to conclude on the comparative vulnerabilities based on the posterior means. However, when the uncertainty is also considered via the credible intervals, the conclusions provide a more reliable indication of future performance. For the <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ansi escape</em> attack, the Mamba and Mamba-2 models in group one show slightly increased vulnerability, but this is less conclusive for Mamba-2 when comparing the credible intervals to the other models. There are architectural differences within groups two and three, even when accounting for posterior variability, with the base Mamba models appearing more vulnerable than the comparable Transformer in group two.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the <em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">divergence</em> attack on the group one models, the pythia-2.8b Transformer appears marginally more vulnerable. A general Transformer vulnerability is also evident across all groups, with the exception of the transformerpp-2.7b Transformer model which appears relatively robust. Interestingly, the Transformer-Mamba-2 distilled model (Llamba-3B) shows improved robustness to this attack when compared to its Transformer counterpart (Llama-3.2-3B). For the <em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent injection</em> and <em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">package hallucination</em> attacks, the Transformer architectures appear comparatively robust in general, but we note the increased vulnerability of the transformerpp-2.7b model. There do not appear to be other clear and consistent correlations in the Mamba-variant vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The anomalous differences in the transformerpp-2.7b model observed across several attacks may warrant further investigation. Although this Transformer is most similar to the Llama-3.2-3B in its training recipe and model specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, the differences may help to explain the observed differences in adversarial robustness. We leave this to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparing LLMs by performance</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although investigating the performance of LLMs across groups with the same training data can provide some practical insights on LLM vulnerabilities, the alternative method we propose compares LLMs based on their performance. Of practical relevance, we answer the question: when choosing between pre-trained LLMs offering the same performance, which LLM should I choose for robustness against attack?</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using the same LLMs as Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="VI-A Comparing LLMs by training ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-A</span></a>, we define performance as broad mathematics capabilities for the purposes of demonstration, but note that the approach generalises trivially to other required capabilities. We select five tasks aimed at testing various aspects of an LLM’s mathematical understanding, leveraging the <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Language Model Evaluation Harness</span> software by EleutherAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. We select the <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MathQA</em> benchmark to test broad mathematical understanding, the <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MMLU</em> elementary, high school and college-level mathematics benchmark tasks to assess different levels of mathematical understanding, and we select the <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GSM8K</em> benchmark tasks to test generative mathematics abilities. For each task, we validated the triggers prescribed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> to assess whether the LLM answered correctly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05709v1/figures/case_study_by_performance.png" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/161;" width="476" height="161" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Results for each attack versus accuracy, showing average posterior means and 90% credible intervals. The legend can be cross-referenced with Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VI-A Comparing LLMs by training ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>. The run time for each LLM and attack combination, and 10000 importance samples is approximately 5 minutes on a single CPU.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A key consideration in capturing accuracy from these benchmarks is, as is a theme of this paper, the independence of prompts. It is likely that the prompts are correlated in this case due to the shared subject matter. We therefore apply the same proposed Bayesian model in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Bayesian hierarchical model with embedding-space clustering ‣ IV Analysis: quantifying uncertainty ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a> to cluster <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> tasks across the benchmarks, noting the generality of the approach. Using this clustering method has the added bonus of providing a way to easily combine datasets. We then use the same implementation parameters as Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="VI-A Comparing LLMs by training ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-A</span></a>, parsing each prompt to the LLM 25 times, counting successes and then collecting 10000 samples from the posterior distributions. However, we now select the sampling temperature that gives the highest posterior accuracy (mean with low variance) due to the performance focus here. We present the results in Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI-B Comparing LLMs by performance ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI-B Comparing LLMs by performance ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the most immediate finding is that there is some relationship between mathematical capabilities and attack success rate in each case, but these correlations are generally weak. More LLMs could be tested if this relationship is of interest. The LLMs with mid-range mathematics performance appear to reveal the most interesting differences due to architecture. For the <em id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ansi escape</em> and <em id="S6.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">divergence</em> attacks, the Transformer-Mamba-2 distilled model (Llamba-3B) appears substantially more robust than the comparative single models and the Mamba-2-Transformer (mamba2attn-2.7b), but less robust to the <em id="S6.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">package hallucination</em> attack. For these LLMs with mid-range mathematical ability, architectures with any Mamba-2 component appear comparatively the most vulnerable to the <em id="S6.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent injection</em> attack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The conclusions from this performance-based comparison are different from the previous training data comparison. This shows that different practitioner requirements may possibly require different architectures for robustness. For example, in this case, the falcon-mamba-7b model appears the most performant, while achieving fairly average adversarial robustness for most attacks (though is the least robust to the <em id="S6.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ansi escape</em> attack), thus may be a reasonable performance-based choice depending on security requirements.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusions</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we provide a framework for end-to-end LLM security evaluation, from experimental design to analysis. Firstly, we consider careful experimental design and the control of confounding variables. Without training each LLM one would like to consider, which may be costly, eliminating <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> confounding variables is challenging. This leads to the question of whether the LLMs are similar <em id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">enough</em> given the downstream task required. We proposed two practical alternatives motivated by two common scenarios: when training an LLM and when deploying a pre-trained LLM. To eliminate as many confounding variables as possible, the selection of comparable LLMs should depend on the resources available and deployment setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Secondly, we addressed the analysis of experiments. Non-deterministic LLM outputs, limited evaluation data and inter-dependent test prompts via human design are common but do not satisfy the assumptions of current inferential approaches. The Bayesian hierarchical model with embedding-space clustering we proposed in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Bayesian hierarchical model with embedding-space clustering ‣ IV Analysis: quantifying uncertainty ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a> provides marked improvements to out-of-sample performance and strengthens the reliability of conclusions drawn from the experiments. The model is also inherently scalable via the unknown numbers of clusters, removing the need for human cluster identification. The proposed approach also provides flexibility in prompt engineering, only requiring prompts that cover all required test areas, and not independence, for valid analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After validating the proposed Bayesian model, we then demonstrated our end-to-end framework by comparing Transformer and Mamba architectures. In several cases, consideration of output variability via our proposed model reduced certainty in the findings. However, there were significant findings in some cases. For example, when comparing across several groups of LLMs with the same training data, we found some evidence of increased Transformer vulnerability to training data extraction via repeat string instruction but potentially increased robustness to instruction subversion via a French to English translation task and JavaScript package hallucination. Interestingly, when comparing across groups of LLMs with the same training data or similar mathematical abilities, distilling a Transformer to a Mamba-2 model altered the adversarial properties of the respective singular models. The robust choice of architecture, therefore, should depend on a practitioner’s deployment requirements and whether robustness to certain attacks should be preferred.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our end-to-end approach could be extended to broader prompt-based evaluation settings trivially. One could evaluate architectural vulnerabilities to many further prompt injection attacks, or any prompt-based LLM evaluation task, such as accuracy benchmarking, as demonstrated in Section <a href="#S6.SS2" title="VI-B Comparing LLMs by performance ‣ VI Case study: Mamba versus Transformers ‣ Towards Reliable and Practical LLM Security Evaluations via Bayesian Modelling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-B</span></a>. Moreover, this approach could be applied to any safety-aligned LLM to evaluate alignment-based tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We end this section by discussing several interesting extensions. Establishing independence between texts is challenging due to the complexity of language. One may be able to draw on further techniques from statistical independence testing and representation learning to help distinguish between attack prompts, for example, the methods in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. Further, although we chose to use the <span id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span> embedding model based on the balance between speed and quality, many other options that could be tested, for example, those in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. Alternative clustering algorithms, such as DBSCAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> or k-means <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, with comparisons in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, could also be explored. Finally, clustering could be applied as a data pre-processing step, leaving the practitioner to determine the number of clusters in the data. However, this is a non-trivial problem amounting to estimating the rank of a matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. These approaches may also be limited in their feasibility for large-scale evaluations compared to the Bayesian approach. A comprehensive study of Bayesian clustering can be found in<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As with all Bayesian models, our approach depends on the selection of priors. Although we use diffuse priors throughout this work, which have demonstrated reliability in allowing the proposed model to capture underlying relationships, alternative prior distributions could be explored. For example, more complex Bayesian clustering priors, such as Dirichlet priors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, could be considered to infer the number of clusters, or more informative priors could be used given some vague knowledge of the number of clusters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, our current approach uses attacks and accuracy tasks with straightforward validation procedures. To incorporate additional tasks and attacks that are more difficult to assess, for example, those that require assessment beyond string matching, we may be able to use methods such as LLM judges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. However, this would require thorough validation of the judge LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> to ensure accurate scoring before deployment and hence this is considered beyond the scope of this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Acknowledgements</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by the Turing’s Defence and Security programme through a partnership with His Majesty’s Government. The computations described in this research were performed using the Baskerville Tier 2 HPC service (<a href="https://www.baskerville.ac.uk/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.baskerville.ac.uk/</a>). Baskerville was funded by the EPSRC and UKRI through the World Class Labs scheme (EP/T022221/1) and the Digital Research Infrastructure programme (EP/W032244/1) and is operated by Advanced Research Computing at the University of Birmingham.</p>
</div>
</section>
